29, Frequency of t is .371, Number of students with TT is 180, Tt is 418, tt is 200\nF. Frequency of T is .604, Frequency of t is .396, Number of students with TT is 373, Tt is 316, tt is 109\nG. Frequency of T is .467, Frequency of t is .533, Number of students with TT is 250, Tt is 298, tt is 250\nH. Frequency of T is .560, Frequency of t is .440, Number of students with TT is 180, Tt is 438, tt is 180\nI. Frequency of T is .467, Frequency of t is .533, Number of students with TT is 109, Tt is 373, tt is 316\nJ. Frequency of T is .629, Frequency of t is .371, Number of students with TT is 316, Tt is 373, tt is 109\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "A group of students were invited to taste phenylthiocarbamide (PTC). The ability to taste PTC is inherited by a single pair of genes and tasting (T) is dominant to non-tasting (t). Among 798 students, 60.4 percent were tasters. (a) Calculate the allelic frequency of T and t. (b) How many of the students were TT, Tt, tt? (a) Frequency of T is 0.371, frequency of t is 0.629, number of students with TT is 109, Tt is 373, tt is 316. (b) Frequency of T is 0.396, frequency of t is 0.604, number of students with TT is 150, Tt is 348, tt is 300. (c) Frequency of T is 0.533, frequency of t is 0.467, number of students with TT is 200, Tt is 398, tt is 200. (d) Frequency of T is 0.440, frequency of t is 0.560, number of students with TT is 220, Tt is 358, tt is 220. (e) Frequency of T is 0.629, frequency of t is 0.371, number of students with TT is 180, Tt is 418, tt is 200. (f) Frequency of T is 0.604, frequency of t is 0.396, number of students with TT is 373, Tt is 316, tt is 109. (g) Frequency is 0.467, frequency of T is 0.533, number of students with TT is 250, Tt is 298, tt is 250. (h) Frequency of T is 0.560, frequency of t is 0.440, number of students with TT is 180, Tt is 438, tt is 180. (i) Frequency of T is 0.467,", "mode": "local", "answer": "B. Frequency of T is 0.396, frequency of t is 0.604; number of students with TT is 150, Tt is 348, tt is 300", "answer_ms": 991}
{"id": "q0208", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "tier": "conservative", "audio_s": 49.17, "n_chunks": 50, "scores": [0.3617, 0.3759, 0.4446, 0.3928, 0.3345, 0.3472, 0.3351, 0.3335, 0.3312, 0.3709, 0.3762, 0.4075, 0.3612, 0.3412, 0.3742, 0.3155, 0.3204, 0.3385, 0.3374, 0.3744, 0.3418, 0.3389, 0.3478, 0.3337, 0.3416, 0.3236, 0.3288, 0.3249, 0.3314, 0.3902, 0.3537, 0.3278, 0.3195, 0.3153, 0.3127, 0.3131, 0.3207, 0.3273, 0.3437, 0.3286, 0.3222, 0.3196, 0.3323, 0.3211, 0.3067, 0.3189, 0.3291, 0.3439, 0.3489, 0.3175], "eot_score": 0.7096, "eot_read_ms": 20, "reference_answer": "C. An assignment that continues the obligor's duty or risk.", "query": "Third-year student is studying contracts. He has learned that generally all contractual rights may be assigned, but there are some exceptions. Which of the following is not an exception?\n\nA. An assignment that substantially changes obligor's duty or risk.\nB. An assignment that changes the nature of the contract.\nC. An assignment that continues the obligor's duty or risk.\nD. An assignment that transfers rights to an incapable party.\nE. An assignment that causes undue hardship or loss to the obligor.\nF. An assignment that reduces the value of the contract to the obligor.\nG. An assignment of future rights from future contracts.\nH. An assignment that is made without the consent of the other party.\nI. An assignment not allowed by law.\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "I'm sorry, but I can't provide a transcription for this audio clip because it appears to be completely silent or contain no discernible speech content.", "mode": "local", "answer": "The **\"best\" way to write a resume** depends on your goals, experience, and industry, but there are proven principles that consistently lead to strong results. Here’s a step-by-step guide to creating an effective resume that gets you noticed by hiring managers and applicant tracking systems ATS effectively, especially for technical roles like software engineering or data science—though these strategies apply broadly across industries too, with slight tweaks for non-technical fields like marketing or sales if needed. For this example, we’ll focus on a **technical candidate**, which aligns with the common use case implied by the context of \"technical skills\" and \"GitHub,\" though many sections can be adapted accordingly.", "answer_ms": 3229}
{"id": "q0217", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "tier": "conservative", "audio_s": 75.05, "n_chunks": 76, "scores": [0.5093, 0.5569, 0.3386, 0.3749, 0.3787, 0.4133, 0.275, 0.2774, 0.424, 0.444, 0.586, 0.6643, 0.4395, 0.0725, 0.5502, 0.2894, 0.4423, 0.275, 0.4273, 0.2053, 0.5074, 0.3347, 0.4439, 0.666, 0.4193, 0.2769, 0.4216, 0.3786, 0.4622, 0.5517, 0.3963, 0.243, 0.2297, 0.3352, 0.4593, 0.3474, 0.3706, 0.3336, 0.364, 0.4356, 0.5366, 0.4812, 0.3601, 0.4012, 0.3296, 0.2942, 0.5992, 0.1093, 0.3734, 0.3209, 0.332, 0.4527, 0.564, 0.5852, 0.3308, 0.3465, 0.2317, 0.4472, 0.3717, 0.3668, 0.3578, 0.528, 0.3677, 0.4045, 0.4088, 0.4299, 0.4056, 0.5323, 0.458, 0.4858, 0.4397, 0.4672, 0.4332, 0.2613, 0.3185, 0.4459], "eot_score": 0.7966, "eot_read_ms": 21, "reference_answer": "C. The belt will not slip at 150° but tends to slip at 100°.", "query": "An engine running at 1100 rpm drives a line shaft by means of a belt. The engine pulley has a 9 in. diameter and 25 hp has to be transmitted. The coefficient of friction y between the belt and pulley material is 0.25. Determine, whether the belt slip is possible for the following angles of contact: (a) 150° (b) 100°\n\nA. The belt will slip at both 150° and 100°.\nB. The belt slip is independent of the angle of contact.\nC. The belt will not slip at 150° but tends to slip at 100°.\nD. The belt will not slip at either angle if the power transmitted is reduced to 20 hp.\nE. The belt will slip at 100° only if the engine speed is increased to 1200 rpm.\nF. The belt will slip at both angles if the coefficient of friction is reduced to 0.20.\nG. The belt will not slip at both 150° and 100°.\nH. The belt will not slip at 100° but tends to slip at 150°.\nI. The belt will slip at 150° if the power transmitted exceeds 30 hp.\nJ. The belt will slip at 150° but not at 100°.\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "An engine running at 1100 rpm drives a line shaft by means of a belt. The engine pulley has a 9-in diameter, and 25 hp has to be transmitted. The coefficient of friction μ between the belt and pulley material is 0.25. Determine whether the belt slip is possible for the following angles of contact: (a) 150° and 100°; (a) The belt will slip at both 150° and 100°. (b) The belt slip is independent of the angle of contact. (c) The belt will not slip at 150° but tends to slip at 100°. (d) The belt will not slip at either angle if the power transmitted is reduced to 20 hp. (e) The belt will slip at 100° only if the engine speed is increased to 1200 rpm. (f) The belt will slip at both angles if the coefficient of friction is reduced to 0.2. (g) The belt will not slip at both 150° and 100°. (h) The belt will not slip at 100° but tends to slip at 150°. (i) The belt will slip at 150° if the power transmitted exceeds 30 hp. (j) The belt will slip at 150° but not at 100°. Answer with the correct option letter and its content.", "mode": "local", "answer": "C. The belt will not slip at 150° but tends to slip at 100°.", "answer_ms": 524}
{"id": "q0225", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "tier": "conservative", "audio_s": 48.7, "n_chunks": 49, "scores": [0.3794, 0.3796, 0.3519, 0.1623, 0.3887, 0.3338, 0.4237, 0.447, 0.45, 0.4051, 0.4293, 0.6099, 0.5022, 0.3823, 0.3666, 0.5663, 0.6204, 0.4862, 0.4995, 0.4374, 0.6248, 0.5111, 0.3546, 0.4593, 0.5003, 0.4428, 0.575, 0.3945, 0.3993, 0.5475, 0.3497, 0.5648, 0.5413, 0.3527, 0.4726, 0.4994, 0.3812, 0.4027, 0.4645, 0.3028, 0.5674, 0.6753, 0.3347, 0.4798, 0.4063, 0.5219, 0.4083, 0.2518, 0.4376], "eot_score": 0.8733, "eot_read_ms": 19, "reference_answer": "B. +7.3$\\mathrm{kJ} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$", "query": "Assume that all gases are perfect and that data refer to 298.15 K unless otherwise stated. Calculate the change in chemical potential of a perfect gas when its pressure is increased isothermally from $1.8 \\mathrm{~atm}$ to $29.5 \\mathrm{~atm}$ at $40^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$.\n\nA. +10.2$\\mathrm{kJ} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$\nB. +7.3$\\mathrm{kJ} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$\nC. +8.4$\\mathrm{kJ} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$\nD. +1.9$\\mathrm{kJ} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$\nE. +5.9$\\mathrm{kJ} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$\nF. +2.6$\\mathrm{kJ} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$\nG. +6.2$\\mathrm{kJ} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$\nH. +4.8$\\mathrm{kJ} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$\nI. +3.5$\\mathrm{kJ} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$\nJ. +9.1$\\mathrm{kJ} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "Assume that all gases are perfect and that data refer to 298.15 K unless otherwise stated. Calculate the change in chemical potential of a perfect gas when its pressure is increased isothermally from 1.8 atm to 29.5 atm at 40°C. (a) +10.2 kJ mol$^{-1}$ (b) +7.3 kJ mol$^{-1}$ (c) -8.4 kJ mol$^{-1}$ (d) +1.9 kJ mol$^{-1}$ (e) +5.9 kJ mol$^{-1}$ (f) -2.6 kJ mol$^{-1}$ (g) -6.2 kJ mol$^{-1}$ (h) +4.8 kJ mol$^{-1}$ (i) -3.4 kJ mol$^{-1}$ (j) -9.1 kJ mol$^{-1}$. Answer with the correct option letter and its content.", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "The change in chemical potential is +7.3 kJ mol⁻¹.", "expert_answer": "(b) +7.3 kJ mol$^{-1}$", "expert_latency_s": 2.09, "stall_ms": 20, "relay_ms": 360}
{"id": "q0235", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "tier": "conservative", "audio_s": 72.44, "n_chunks": 73, "scores": [0.4022, 0.3527, 0.3275, 0.3988, 0.5416, 0.355, 0.3734, 0.3308, 0.2804, 0.4042, 0.2965, 0.5891, 0.2405, 0.3014, 0.2042, 0.2025, 0.324, 0.314, 0.2863, 0.2026, 0.1928, 0.216, 0.2424, 0.2319, 0.2639, 0.3519, 0.3501, 0.3889, 0.2616, 0.2459, 0.4094, 0.3706, 0.373, 0.3595, 0.3381, 0.2589, 0.4021, 0.371, 0.4448, 0.4163, 0.3687, 0.3458, 0.2879, 0.398, 0.3653, 0.3959, 0.4068, 0.3924, 0.1905, 0.289, 0.5105, 0.4642, 0.4808, 0.4134, 0.3338, 0.4687, 0.3394, 0.3391, 0.2856, 0.3146, 0.3344, 0.2738, 0.4314, 0.4443, 0.4472, 0.3999, 0.3551, 0.3386, 0.4116, 0.3369, 0.3028, 0.257, 0.4204], "eot_score": 0.4496, "eot_read_ms": 20, "reference_answer": "G. Burials with exotic grave goods also contain bones indicating that they engaged in less strenuous labor.", "query": "Which of the following is archaeological evidence of social stratification and inequality?\n\nA. Burials with exotic grave goods also contain bones indicating that they engaged in more strenuous labor.\nB. Burials all contain equal amounts of exotic grave goods, but some are more impressive than others.\nC. Burials without exotic grave goods also contain bones indicating that they engaged in strenuous labor.\nD. Burials all contain bones indicating that they engaged in same level of strenuous labor.\nE. Burials contain bones indicating that people were segregated based on gender, stature, and personal capabilities, including artistic abilities.\nF. All burials contain equal amounts of non-exotic grave goods.\nG. Burials with exotic grave goods also contain bones indicating that they engaged in less strenuous labor.\nH. Burials contain bones indicating that people were segregated based on their age and health.\nI. Burials without exotic grave goods also contain bones indicating that they engaged in less strenuous labor.\nJ. Burials contain only exotic grave goods with no indication of the deceased's labor.\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "Which of the following is archaeological evidence of social stratification and inequality? (A) Burials with exotic grave goods also contain bones indicating that they engaged in more strenuous labor. (B) Burials all contain equal amounts of exotic grave goods but some are more impressive than others. (C) Burials without exotic grave goods also contain bones indicating that they engaged in strenuous labor. (D) Burials all contain bones indicating that they engaged in same level of strenuous labor. (E) Burials contain bones indicating that people were segregated based on gender, stature, and personal capabilities, including artistic abilities. (F) All burials contain equal amounts of non-exotic grave goods. (G) Burials with exotic grave goods also contain bones indicating that they engaged in less strenuous labor. (H) Burials contain bones indicating that people were segregated based on their age and health. (I) Burials without exotic grave goods also contain bones indicating that they engaged in less strenuous labor. (J) Burials contain only exotic grave goods with no indication of the deceased's labor. Answer with the correct option letter and its content.", "mode": "local", "answer": "B. Burials all contain exotic grave goods, but some are more impressive than others.", "answer_ms": 399}
{"id": "q0241", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "tier": "conservative", "audio_s": 37.66, "n_chunks": 38, "scores": [0.395, 0.2736, 0.2685, 0.3367, 0.2661, 0.5085, 0.3134, 0.3207, 0.257, 0.3059, 0.3873, 0.4341, 0.3172, 0.3185, 0.3359, 0.3319, 0.4132, 0.3654, 0.3904, 0.3659, 0.3397, 0.4412, 0.448, 0.3083, 0.3509, 0.3473, 0.3316, 0.3028, 0.3627, 0.4009, 0.4093, 0.4462, 0.4163, 0.3989, 0.3525, 0.3403, 0.3825, 0.4293], "eot_score": 0.5165, "eot_read_ms": 22, "reference_answer": "D. Economic, Legal, Ethical and Philanthropic", "query": "According Carroll what four categories or aspects can corporate social responsibility be broken down into?\n\nA. Political, Legal, Ethical and Philanthropic\nB. Economic, Legal, Ethical and Moral\nC. Economic, Legal, Ethical and Environmental\nD. Economic, Legal, Ethical and Philanthropic\nE. Political, Legal, Cultural and Philanthropic\nF. Economic, Legal, Humanitarian and Philanthropic\nG. Economic, Legal, Cultural and Philanthropic\nH. Economic, Cultural, Ethical and Environmental\nI. Political, Legal, Economic and Environmental\nJ. Political, Legal, Ethical and Environmental\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "According to Carroll, what four categories or aspects can corporate social responsibility be broken down into? A) political, legal, ethical, and philanthropic. B) economic, legal, ethical, and moral. C) economic, legal, ethical, and environmental. D) economic, legal, ethical, and philanthropic. E) political, legal, cultural, and philanthropic. F) economic, legal, humanitarian, and philanthropic. G) economic, legal, cultural, and philanthropic. H) economic, cultural, ethical, and environmental. I) political, legal, economic, and environmental. J) political, legal, ethical, and environmental. Answer with the correct option letter and its content.", "mode": "local", "answer": "The correct answer is C. economic, legal, ethical, and environmental.", "answer_ms": 328}
{"id": "q0247", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "tier": "conservative", "audio_s": 67.31, "n_chunks": 68, "scores": [0.4607, 0.384, 0.2874, 0.4018, 0.166, 0.5226, 0.446, 0.4142, 0.2805, 0.3675, 0.4298, 0.4875, 0.3003, 0.3251, 0.2585, 0.2152, 0.2361, 0.3631, 0.3662, 0.3228, 0.2966, 0.4057, 0.2801, 0.4075, 0.2923, 0.3041, 0.2301, 0.1672, 0.0703, 0.3646, 0.3925, 0.3612, 0.2791, 0.3561, 0.319, 0.3534, 0.3912, 0.3997, 0.3616, 0.3756, 0.331, 0.3493, 0.2905, 0.2861, 0.3234, 0.4575, 0.3643, 0.3703, 0.3007, 0.2927, 0.3951, 0.4782, 0.2945, 0.3224, 0.3498, 0.2597, 0.4646, 0.5329, 0.3593, 0.3669, 0.3194, 0.2878, 0.3454, 0.3418, 0.3682, 0.2368, 0.1492, 0.3819], "eot_score": 0.3874, "eot_read_ms": 19, "reference_answer": "B. Parallel storage allows simultaneous storage of information, activating it from different sources after an experience and contributing to both memory and thinking.", "query": "Why are parallel memory codes so important in the understanding of the role of memory in thought?\n\nA. Parallel storage duplicates information across different brain regions, leading to multiple identical memories\nB. Parallel storage allows simultaneous storage of information, activating it from different sources after an experience and contributing to both memory and thinking.\nC. Parallel storage enables the brain to process information sequentially rather than simultaneously\nD. Parallel storage helps in storing large volumes of data\nE. Parallel storage helps in quickly retrieving information\nF. Parallel storage helps in organizing information\nG. Parallel storage increases the brain's reliance on single-threaded processing\nH. Parallel storage compresses data for compact storage, but does not affect thinking\nI. Parallel storage ensures complete accuracy in memory recall without any distortions\nJ. Parallel storage is important for creating long-term memories, but has no impact on immediate thought processes\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "Why are parallel memory codes so important in the understanding of the role of memory and thought? A. Parallel storage duplicates information across different brain regions, leading to multiple identical memories. B. Parallel storage allows simultaneous storage of information, activating it from different sources after an experience and contributing to both memory and thinking. C. Parallel storage enables the brain to process information sequentially rather than simultaneously. D. Parallel storage helps in storing large volumes of data. E. Parallel storage helps in quickly retrieving information. F. Parallel storage helps in organizing information. G. Parallel storage increases the brain's reliance on single-threaded processing. H. Parallel storage compresses data for compact storage but does not affect thinking. I. Parallel storage ensures complete accuracy in memory recall without any distortions. J. Parallel storage is important for creating long-term memories but has no impact on immediate thought processes. Answer with the correct option letter and its content.", "mode": "local", "answer": "B. Parallel storage allows simultaneous storage of information, activating it from different sources after an experience and contributing to both memory and thinking.", "answer_ms": 528}
{"id": "q0255", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "tier": "conservative", "audio_s": 77.4, "n_chunks": 78, "scores": [0.4217, 0.4344, 0.4768, 0.507, 0.4786, 0.3187, 0.2794, 0.3707, 0.3487, 0.2769, 0.3721, 0.3639, 0.4248, 0.2713, 0.2755, 0.3082, 0.2164, 0.178, 0.1202, 0.2049, 0.3899, 0.1568, 0.152, 0.275, 0.2155, 0.1898, 0.2575, 0.295, 0.3324, 0.3676, 0.4344, 0.3865, 0.4857, 0.348, 0.377, 0.215, 0.2667, 0.3231, 0.3135, 0.3563, 0.5027, 0.4568, 0.5485, 0.3044, 0.3228, 0.2778, 0.3377, 0.2654, 0.4725, 0.3201, 0.3523, 0.4228, 0.4909, 0.3487, 0.307, 0.3423, 0.412, 0.415, 0.4029, 0.376, 0.3589, 0.4328, 0.3188, 0.3185, 0.3585, 0.4482, 0.4409, 0.456, 0.3886, 0.3399, 0.377, 0.3554, 0.4515, 0.4017, 0.3993, 0.3549, 0.308, 0.4437], "eot_score": 0.3789, "eot_read_ms": 21, "reference_answer": "I. admissible as an admission.", "query": "A college student and a man got into a fight at a bar while playing pooi. The college student allegedly started the fight when he struck the man in the head with a pool cue after the man sank a game-winning shot. At the college student's trial for assault, the prosecution called one of the college student's classmates to testify. The classmate was not at the bar during the fight. The classmate offered to testify that the next day at class, he approached the college student and said, \"It was wrong of you to start a fight with the man over a game of pool,\" at which point, the college student bowed his head and walked away. The classmate's testimony is\n\nA. admissible as a statement of the declarant’s then-existing state of mind.\nB. inadmissible as a character evidence.\nC. inadmissible as hearsay not within any recognized exception.\nD. admissible as a prior consistent statement.\nE. inadmissible as a statement that is not in the defendant's favor.\nF. inadmissible because the classmate was not an eyewitness to the fight.\nG. inadmissible as self-serving.\nH. admissible as part of the res gestae.\nI. admissible as an admission.\nJ. admissible as a statement of personal or family history.\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "A college student and a man got into a fight at a bar while playing pool. The college student allegedly started the fight when he struck the man in the head with a pool cue after the man sank a game-winning shot. At the college student's trial for assault, the prosecution called one of the college student's classmates to testify. The classmate was not at the bar during the fight. The classmate offered to testify that the next day at class, he approached the college student and said, \"It was wrong of you to start a fight with the man over a game of pool,\" at which point the college student bowed his head and walked away. The classmate's testimony is A) admissible as a statement of the declarant's then-existing state of mind. B) inadmissible as character evidence. C) inadmissible as hearsay not within any recognized exception. D) admissible as a prior consistent statement. E) inadmissible as a statement that is not in the defendant's favor. F) inadmissible because the classmate was not an eyewitness to the fight. G) inadmissible as self-serving. H) admissible as part of the res gestae. I) admissible as an admission. J) admissible as a statement of personal or family history. Answer with the correct option letter and its content.", "mode": "local", "answer": "A. admissible as a statement of the declarant's then-existing state of mind.", "answer_ms": 400}
{"id": "q0264", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "tier": "conservative", "audio_s": 150.71, "n_chunks": 151, "scores": [0.3828, 0.3117, 0.3424, 0.3656, 0.4176, 0.3898, 0.3527, 0.4163, 0.4212, 0.3278, 0.3012, 0.4283, 0.297, 0.2541, 0.3427, 0.5733, 0.3061, 0.2849, 0.4615, 0.4224, 0.3754, 0.3224, 0.3871, 0.4345, 0.3846, 0.2189, 0.4036, 0.7133, 0.3355, 0.3502, 0.356, 0.3498, 0.3568, 0.4153, 0.2498, 0.3094, 0.3919, 0.4609, 0.5183, 0.4166, 0.3768, 0.3745, 0.4083, 0.2825, 0.3992, 0.401, 0.5408, 0.2868, 0.4422, 0.2501, 0.3036, 0.4552, 0.4381, 0.3608, 0.2903, 0.2861, 0.3831, 0.3079, 0.4552, 0.5184, 0.685, 0.4116, 0.4209, 0.3215, 0.3989, 0.461, 0.5211, 0.2373, 0.494, 0.4416, 0.3413, 0.3059, 0.4434, 0.3505, 0.3349, 0.3723, 0.4159, 0.3165, 0.3776, 0.4494, 0.4899, 0.3853, 0.3763, 0.3973, 0.3991, 0.3505, 0.6283, 0.4666, 0.394, 0.6242, 0.3677, 0.7803, 0.4785, 0.4127, 0.42, 0.3579, 0.3023, 0.3376, 0.6646, 0.5478, 0.4112, 0.474, 0.5631, 0.4874, 0.3284, 0.3597, 0.4028, 0.2948, 0.3163, 0.2898, 0.3574, 0.377, 0.3487, 0.4012, 0.3937, 0.4554, 0.4576, 0.5793, 0.4759, 0.3685, 0.3436, 0.4358, 0.36, 0.3594, 0.4485, 0.4922, 0.4679, 0.449, 0.4041, 0.4008, 0.2951, 0.3769, 0.3863, 0.4069, 0.3983, 0.3313, 0.3535, 0.5094, 0.4425, 0.4931, 0.4508, 0.5176, 0.3989, 0.5216, 0.3574, 0.4646, 0.4979, 0.4225, 0.2707, 0.6227, 0.4445], "eot_score": 0.7794, "eot_read_ms": 21, "reference_answer": "B. not recover, because the professor remains liable.", "query": "A landlord owned a three-story home in fee simple and leased it to his friend for a term of 10 years. By the terms of the lease, the friend expressly covenanted to pay a monthly rental of $300 and to pay the taxes on the premises during the term of the lease. The lease also stipulated that the friend, as lessee, may not assign or sublease the said premises. The friend and his family lived in the house for two years. Then the friend, still owing the landlord three months back rent, assigned his leasehold interest in the property to a professor, who was unaware of the prohibition against assignments. This written assignment expressly provided that the professor would pay the landlord the monthly rental of $300, but was silent concerning the payment of taxes. The landlord never objected to this assignment. Six months after the assignment, the landlord conveyed his property interest in the premises to a new owner. After residing in the house for a year, the professor subleased the third floor to a student for a term of two years; the agreement provided that the professor would receive a monthly rental payment of $100. After the student had made regular rental payments to the professor for the first six months of the sublease, he defaulted. The following month, the professor sent the new owner $200 as payment for rent and notified him that he should collect the remaining $100 from the student, who refused to vacate the premises. In an action by the new owner against the student to recover the additional $100 for rent due, he will most probably\n\nA. recover, even though he never objected to the sublease.\nB. not recover, because the professor remains liable.\nC. recover, because the new owner inherited all rights and responsibilities from the original landlord.\nD. recover, because the landlord and sublessee are in privity of estate.\nE. not recover, because the student is not directly responsible to the new owner.\nF. not recover, because the student was not in privity of contract with the new owner.\nG. not recover, because the professor would only be held liable.\nH. not recover, because the professor is the one who subleased to the student.\nI. recover, because the student is in direct violation of his sublease agreement.\nJ. recover, because the student defaulted on his rental payment.\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "A landlord owned a three-story home in fee simple and leased it to his friend for a term of ten years. By the terms of the lease, the friend expressly covenanted to pay a monthly rental of three hundred dollars and to pay the taxes on the premises during the term of the lease. The lease also stipulated that the friend, as lessee, may not assign or sublease the said premises. The friend and his family lived in the house for two years. Then the friend, still owing the landlord three months back rent, assigned his leasehold interest in the property to a professor, who was unaware of the prohibition against assignments. This written assignment expressly provided that the professor would pay the landlord the monthly rental of three hundred dollars, but was silent concerning the payment of taxes. The landlord never objected to this assignment. Six months after the assignment, the landlord conveyed his property interest in the premises to a new owner. After residing in the house for a year, the professor subleased the third floor to a student for a term of two years. The agreement provided that the professor would receive a monthly rental payment of one hundred dollars. After the student had made regular rental payments to the professor for the first six months of the sublease, he defaulted. The following month, the professor sent the new owner two hundred dollars as payment for rent and notified him that he should collect the remaining one hundred dollars from the student, who refused to vacate the premises. In an action by the new owner against the student to recover the additional one hundred dollars for rent due, he will most probably A) recover, even though he never objected to the sublease. B) not recover, because the professor remains liable. C) recover, because the new owner inherited all rights and responsibilities from the original landlord. D) recover, because the landlord and sublessee are in privity of estate. E) not recover, because the student is not directly responsible to the new owner. F) not recover, because the student was not in privity of contract with the new owner. G) not recover, because the professor would only be held liable. H) not recover, because the professor is the one who subleased to the student. I) recover, because the student is in direct violation of his sublease agreement. J) recover, because the student defaulted on his rental payment.", "mode": "local", "answer": "F. Not recover, because the student was not in privity of contract with the new owner.", "answer_ms": 442}
